<% season = CurrentSeason.year %>
<% last_season = season.to_i - 1 %>
<% content_for :title, "Commissioner's Corner" %>
<% content_for :page_heading, "Commissioner's Corner #{season}" %>

<%# Unlike most pages this one runs the full width, with no sidebar logo, and its text isn't the site's purple. %>
<div class="ptfgPlainText">
  <p style="margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: larger">
    Welcome back, PTFG <b>Private League Commissioners</b>!
  </p>
  <div class="alert alert-danger" role="alert" style="clear: both; max-width: 1000px">
    Please take a few minutes to read everything on the page below — there are several important updates for the
    <%= season %> season including:<br><br>
    <ol style="margin-left: 5px">
      <li>
        There are no <b>Hawaii</b> tournaments this year so the <%= season %> PGA Tour season starts on January 21st
        this year.
      </li>
      <li>
        For the <b>TOUR Championship</b> season finale, PTFG has always used standard pro-rated earnings rather than the
        inflated FedEx Cup finale purse (e.g., $40 million). The PGA Tour’s major overhaul to a two-week, match-play
        format won't take effect until <b>2028</b>, so the 2027 season will remain unaffected. When the new format (and
        location) are fully announced, I plan to make adjustments similar to those I previously used to accommodate the
        Dell Match Play tournament.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <div class="card ptfgPanel">
    <div class="card-header ptfgBluePanelBackground">
      <h3 class="card-title"><b><%= season %> League Setup – Schedule, Settings & Payment</b></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body ccSetup">
      <p class="ccSetup-lead">
        I can only begin setting up your league for the <%= season %> season once you provide your desired tournament
        schedule, number of segments, and any other changes. You can view the <%= season %> schedule
        <%= link_to 'here', "https://www.pgatour.com/schedule/#{season}", target: 'schedule' %>.
      </p>
      <div class="ccNote">
        <i class="bi bi-info-circle ccNote-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>
        <div>
          Keep in mind that PTFG features only <b>one PGA TOUR tournament per week</b>, always selecting the
          <b>most prestigious event</b> when multiple tournaments are scheduled. The 4
          <i>opposite-field/alternate events</i> on the <%= season %> schedule will not be available, as supporting
          them would require significant website changes and could create unnecessary confusion for participants.
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="ccSection">
        <h4 class="ccSection-title"><i class="bi bi-calendar-event" aria-hidden="true"></i>Tournament Schedule</h4>
        <p>When submitting your schedule, please double-check that it reflects exactly what you want for <%= season %>:</p>
        <div class="ccOptions">
          <div class="ccOption ccOption--blue">
            <div class="ccOption-title"><i class="bi bi-arrow-repeat" aria-hidden="true"></i>Keeping the same format?</div>
            <p>
              Simply reply <b>Same as last year</b>. This means retaining the same number of tournaments and segments,
              along with the same starting and ending tournaments as <%= last_season %>, with the exception of the
              <i>Sony Open</i>, which is not being played in <%= season %>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="ccOption ccOption--green">
            <div class="ccOption-title"><i class="bi bi-trophy" aria-hidden="true"></i>Played the Tournament of Champions last year?</div>
            <p>Please let me know how you'd like to adjust your schedule to account for that event.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="ccOption ccOption--red">
            <div class="ccOption-title"><i class="bi bi-pencil-square" aria-hidden="true"></i>Want to make changes?</div>
            <p>
              Include the specific tournaments, segment structure, or other adjustments you'd like for <%= season %>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="ccHint">
          For reference, you can review last season's schedule by selecting <b><%= last_season %></b> on your league's
          <b>Schedule</b> page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="ccSection">
        <h4 class="ccSection-title"><i class="bi bi-sliders" aria-hidden="true"></i>League Settings</h4>
        <p>
          Please also review your current <b>League Settings</b>, located under the <b>Home</b> tab on your league
          website. If you'd like to make any changes, let me know <b>before I set up your <%= season %> season</b>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="ccSection">
        <h4 class="ccSection-title"><i class="bi bi-credit-card" aria-hidden="true"></i>League Fees &amp; Payment</h4>
        <p>
          As in previous years, payment is due <b>one month after your league season begins</b>. By that time, you
          should have your final team count. League pricing remains unchanged for <%= season %>:
        </p>
        <div class="ccPrices">
          <div class="ccPrice">
            <span class="ccPrice-amount"><%= MIN_LEAGUE_FEE %></span>
            <span class="ccPrice-label"><b>Minimum league fee</b>, covering up to 30 teams</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ccPrice">
            <span class="ccPrice-amount">$6</span>
            <span class="ccPrice-label"><b>Per additional team</b>, beyond the first 30</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p>You can review your league's payment information and calculate the total cost under:</p>
        <div class="ccPath">
          <b>Commish</b><i class="bi bi-chevron-right" aria-hidden="true"></i><b>PTFG Payment</b>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="ccSection">
        <h4 class="ccSection-title"><i class="bi bi-stars" aria-hidden="true"></i>What's New for <%= season %>?</h4>
        <p>
          Interested in learning more about the changes to next year's PGA TOUR schedule? Check out the PGA TOUR's
          <%= link_to "#{season} schedule announcement", 'https://www.pgatour.com/article/news/latest/2026/08/26/pga-tour-announces-2027-schedule-through-tour-championship/',
                      :target => '_pgaAnnouncement' %>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card ptfgPanel">
    <div class="card-header ptfgGreenPanelBackground">
      <h3 class="card-title"><b>What's New For <%= season %></b></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ol>
        <li>
          The biggest change this year is that <b>PTFG has integrated with a third-party email delivery service</b> to
          help ensure that messages from our website reach participants’ inboxes. This is a <b>paid service</b> and not
          an inexpensive one. I’ve chosen <b>not to increase the league entry fee</b> this year to cover the added cost,
          though that may need to change next season. It really depends on whether leagues continue to grow — if
          more participants join, the <b>additional $5 per player</b> could help offset the expense. No pressure, of
          course — just putting it out there!
          <br>
          <br>
          As a league commissioner, it’s important to understand that when you send emails through the PTFG website,
          any replies from your league members will now go directly to the <b>personal email address</b> you use to
          login to PTFG. The previously used <i>Commissioner@{YourLeague}.ProTourFantasyGolf.com</i> addresses are
          <b>no longer supported</b> due to many email providers blocking messages sent from the PTFG domain.
          Now that all emails are being sent through a <b>reputable third-party email delivery service</b>, this
          issue should be resolved — as long as participants <b>don’t mark PTFG emails as spam</b>.
          Please be sure you and your league participants review the additional details on your league website on
          how this will work going forward:<br><br>
          <b>Home</b> (tab) &#8594; <b>How Website Emails Work</b>
        </li>
        <br>
        <li>
          No <b>Tournament of Champions</b> tournament so the <%= season %> PGA Tour season starts a week later
          than usual.
          The Tournament of Champions will not be played this year due to drought conditions resulting in the
          <%= season %>  PGA TOUR season starting <b>one week later than usual</b>.
        </li>
        <br>
        <li>
          The <b>TOUR Championship</b> removed the starting-strokes format halfway through the 2025 season and now
          operates like a standard 30-player event. If your league previously skipped it because of the old scoring
          system, it may be worth adding back—this event really is the Super Bowl of the fantasy golf year.
          <span class="ptfgRed">
            PTFG does not use the massive FedEx Cup bonus payout given to the tournament champion
          </span>.
          Instead, the prize pool matches the purse from the two prior FedEx events and is allocated on a pro-rated
          basis. For <%= season.to_i %>, both preceding tournaments have a $20M purse, so the winner receives
          the usual 18% share, or $3.6M. The TOUR Championship uses the same pro-rated structure. Full earnings for
          the 30-player field are available
          <%= link_to 'here', 'https://oneanddone1.protourfantasygolf.com/schedule/purse_breakdown/507',
            :target =>  "_blank" %>
        </li>
        <br>
        <li>
          For 2026, I've decided to remove all advertising from the PTFG site and make the previously
          optional $1 per team ad-free fee mandatory for all leagues. As a result, the site fee is now <b>$6 per
          team,</b>with a league minimum of <b>$180</b>. I made this change after noticing that Google ads have become
          increasingly deceptive, with some misleading users into entering credit card information because they believe
          they are paying a league sign-up fee, along with other confusing tactics. Removing ads entirely eliminates
          this risk and provides a cleaner, safer experience for everyone. Many leagues were already paying the $1 fee
          to disable ads, so this change will have no impact for them. For the remaining leagues, it represents a small
          increase. This decision wasn’t made lightly. It is my first price increase in 19 seasons, and during that time
          my costs for server hosting, monitoring, firewall protection, and software licensing have risen significantly.
          I’ve been able to absorb those increases as leagues have grown, but this season I also added a dedicated
          email delivery service to ensure site-generated emails reliably reach participants’ inboxes. Due to the
          volume of emails sent over a typical 30+ week season, this will add several thousand dollars in annual costs.
          In the past, running ads on league websites that were willing to have them helped offset my operating costs.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="card ptfgPanel">
    <div class="card-header ptfgRedPanelBackground">
      <h3 class="card-title"><b>Reminders &amp; Other Tips</b></h3>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <ol>
        <li>
          <b>Pre-Loading With Last Year's Teams</b><br>
          Don’t forget, you have the option to pre-load your league with the same teams from last year. When you click
          the <b>Pre-Load Teams</b> button, each returning participant will receive a <b>Welcome Back</b> email
          notifying them that a new <%= season %>  team has been created for them, using the same team name as
          the previous season. If there are any participants from last year who should not receive a new team,
          simply mark them as <b>Unsubscribed</b> under the User Settings tab before running the pre-load process.
          <br><br>
          This is the quickest way to get your league set up for the new season. It also ensures that league members
          continue using the same email address as last year for their Login ID. If they register with a different email
          address, they may accidentally create a new user account, which can cause confusion when they log in with
          their old email and don’t see their <%= season %> team.
          <br><br>
          <div class="ptfgRed">
            <b>IMPORTANT:</b><br>
            You should use the Pre-Load Teams feature before creating any new teams, as it becomes <b>unavailable</b>
            once the first <%= season %> team has been created. You can access this option from:
            <br>
            <b>Commish</b> (tab) &#8594; <b>Pre-Load Teams</b>
          </div>
          <br>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Commissioner's Cup</b><br>
          The <b>Commissioner's Cup</b>, introduced a few years ago, has remained one of our most popular optional
          contests. In this side competition, the league commissioner selects a subset of tournaments (minimum of four)
          to form a separate standings race. It’s similar to the Majors Contest, except the chosen tournaments don’t
          have to be majors. For example, if your league begins in early January, you might select all eight signature
          events with elevated purses for your Commissioner's Cup. Or, if you prefer to make the smaller events more
          meaningful, you could choose tournaments with the lowest purses instead. The selection is entirely up to
          you — hence the name Commissioner’s Cup. Up to five prizes can be awarded for this contest, and as with other
          competitions, virtual trophies are given to both the first- and last-place finishers. If your league wants
          to participate in the Commissioner’s Cup, just contact me to have the feature enabled. I’ll also need the
          list of tournaments you’d like to include.
          <br><br>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Save Yourself Some Agony</b><br>
          Don’t be one of those commissioners still chasing down league payments after the season ends. Throughout the
          season, you can use the website’s email filter to message members who haven’t yet paid their league fee.
          And if you really need to get their attention, remember the <b>lineup lockout</b> feature — it allows you to
          prevent unpaid members from submitting their weekly lineups until their fees are paid. It’s an effective last
          resort for dealing with any stragglers. You can find this option under:<br><br>
          <b>Commish</b> (tab) &#8594; <b>Users</b> &#8594; <b>With <%= season %> Team</b> &#8594;
          <b>Edit</b> &#8594; <b>Lineup Lockout</b> (checkbox)
          <br><br>
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Looking for more participants?</b><br>
          I have players in my $25 public leagues who are interested in joining leagues with higher entry fees and
          payouts. If your league is looking to add teams, I’d be happy to post a notice in those leagues and direct
          interested players to contact you through your league’s website (I won’t be sharing your email address). If
          that sounds helpful, just let me know. <b>A couple of requirements are that you must be posting your league
          prizes via the website each season and paying them out in a timely manner.</b>
          <br><br>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
